            <ArticleTitle>Quality of life after treatment of gallstones: results of a randomised study of lithotripsy and open cholecystectomy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>613-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To compare quality of life after extracorporeal shock wave lithotripsy (ESWL) and open cholecystectomy for symptomatic gallstones.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Randomised study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">University hospital, The Netherlands.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUBJECTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">49 Patients, 23 of whom were randomized to undergo ESWL and 26 to undergo open cholecystectomy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Health questionnaires were filled out before, and at 3, 6 and 12 months after treatment.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURE" NlmCategory="METHODS">Improvements of quality of life after one year.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Both treatments improved quality of life. Open cholecystectomy improved quality of life significantly better than ESWL (median percentage gains in Nottingham Health Profile scores at 1 year 17.1 and 10.5, respectively, p = 0.003).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Open cholecystectomy is superior to ESWL in improving quality of life, so cholecystectomy remains the treatment of choice for symptomatic gallstones. For patients who are unfit or unwilling to undergo operation, however, ESWL is adequate because most patients have some benefit from it in terms of quality of life.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
